WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Start with the evidence. B3000 appears in the original elevation template. Its catalog entry identifies a cabinet family; the drawing still needs its own dimensions and quantities.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
One code. One family. The exact B3000 match gives one upper drawer, two hinged doors and an adjustable shelf. Each fact points back to the catalog.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
That second drawer matters. B3100 has two drawers side by side. Similar codes and silhouettes are not enough: the catalog diagram resolves the difference.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Look inside the match. Open the doors, pull out the drawers and separate the parts. Both families include one adjustable shelf beneath the drawer row.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
A code is not a dimension. This study uses a catalog-allowed 36 × 34 × 24 inch variant. Change the width to explore options; an installed size still needs drawing evidence.

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Carry the evidence forward. A useful draft BOM carries source references and unresolved fields. A repeated callout is not automatically another cabinet; quantity and price need their own evidence.
